Jonathan writes:
|> Perhaps it's because I missed the discussion on NTS-L, but I don't see
|> why \charset\ would have to be a primitive. Couldn't it be a macro
|> that resets \catcode, \uccode, and \lccode\ values and selects a set
|> of fonts for which a vf provides the appropriate remapping of the
|> glyph set?

The discussion on NTS-L (as I recall it) stopped pretty dead after
establishing that more than one character set was desirable.  At first
glance, Jonathan's proposal seems to me workable, but it's the sort of
thing I would like to spend some time thinking about.

I recall from the argument on NTS-L that there were things that _I_
thought were outside the scope of the things TeX can currently do.
One trivial example is \uccode{\ss} being {SS}, but that's old hat; I
think there were more.

Robin

